WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.440
Biosecurity researchers measure
catastrophic risk with a specific baseline, the

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.480
annual probability of a human-caused
epidemic killing over 100,000 people

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.680
or causing over $1 trillion in damages.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.000
Historically, that baseline risk sits at
about 0.3% per year.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.040
This chart visualizes the 0.3% baseline.

6
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.000
A 2025 Forecasting Research Institute
survey of experts and superforecasters

7
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.560
found that if AI reaches virologist-level
capabilities, notice the spiker,

8
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.320
the risk multiplies by 5, jumping to 1.5%.

9
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.640
To counter this, experts propose two
primary safeguards, DNA screening

10
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.000
and AI guardrails.

11
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.840
They estimate this slashes the spiked risk
by 70%, returning

12
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:44.880
it to baseline.

13
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.200
This 70% reduction figure creates a
dangerous illusion of safety.

14
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.560
It masks the reality that our primary
biological defenses are

15
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.920
obsolete, and neither of those safeguards
actually works against the

16
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.600
threats they are meant to stop.

17
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.080
The safety predictions of these experts
rely on a timeline

18
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.840
that has already collapsed.

19
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.240
When surveyed, those biosecurity
professionals predicted that AI would not

20
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.560
match top human virologists until sometime
after 2030.

21
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.400
Subsequent baselining studies found that
current large language models have

22
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.160
already crossed that virologist-level
capability threshold today.

23
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.400
This six-year forecasting failure by top
experts means we can

24
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.920
no longer build governance frameworks that
rely on accurate predictions

25
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.360
of AI timelines.

26
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.440
We have to assume surprise.

27
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.120
Current nucleic acid screening is built
for a previous era.

28
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:38.040
When someone orders custom DNA, providers
check that sequence against

29
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.760
list-based databases of known historical
pathogens, like the Select Agents

30
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:47.160
List. That matching process is useless
against modern biological design

31
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.920
tools like the Evo series.

32
00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.160
These models generate novel proteins and
genetic sequences from scratch.

33
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.880
Because the resulting sequence has no
natural analog, it won't

34
00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.720
match anything on existing lists, sailing
through screening without raising

35
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.600
an alarm. Even for known threats that
appear on those

36
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.320
lists, the screening is easily bypassed.

37
00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.920
Adversaries can split a dangerous gene
into fragments shorter than

38
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.680
the standard 50 to 200 base pair detection
threshold, order

39
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.080
them separately, and reassemble them
later.

40
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.160
Another documented technique interleaves
those toxic gene fragments with benign

41
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.000
sequences. Once introduced into a target
cell, the cell's own

42
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.680
internal splicing machinery cuts out the
benign parts and reassembles

43
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.680
the harmful agent for the attacker.

44
00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.080
List-based screening fails because it
searches for historical structural matches

45
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.680
instead of analyzing the actual biological
function of the sequence.

46
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.480
It looks for shapes we recognize while
ignoring what the

47
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:41.680
sequence will do.

48
00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.440
The upstream defense, the safety
guardrails baked into AI models,

49
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.760
shares a similar structural weakness.

50
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.920
These refusal mechanisms are statistical.

51
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:55.240
patterns They lack the robustness of
hard-coded security architectures.

52
00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:00.360
Researchers regularly achieve up to 92%
success rates bypassing these

53
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.800
guardrails using persuasive adversarial
prompts.

54
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.200
Simply translating the malicious request
into a low-resource language where

55
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:13.000
safety training is sparse increases the
bypass rate by 62%.

56
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.600
For specialized biological models,
attackers use automated AI-on-AI frameworks

57
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.080
like GeneBreaker. This allows one AI to
attack another, successfully

58
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:27.320
generating pathogenic sequences for
viruses with up to 60% success

59
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:32.200
rates. Refusal mechanisms reliably break
under adversarial pressure.

60
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:36.120
Treating AI safety training as a primary
security wall leaves

61
00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.360
the entire system exposed.

62
00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.120
AI systems attack both ends of the
biosecurity spectrum.

63
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.000
Large language models lower the floor for
bioterrorism by converting

64
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.720
tacit laboratory knowledge into explicit
interactive troubleshooting, expanding the threat

65
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.080
pool to novices.

66
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.040
Biological design tools raise the ceiling.

67
00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.040
Instead of reproducing known threats, they
allow operators to design

68
00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.360
entirely new agents optimized for
virulence and immune evasion.

69
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.280
These risks compound.

70
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.280
A novice uses a language model to acquire
foundational virology,

71
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.720
operates a biological design tool to
invent a novel agent,

72
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.280
and then returns to the language model to
troubleshoot the

73
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.120
physical laboratory culturing.

74
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:18.800
These risks multiply.

75
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.640
Every new capability and biological design
increases the danger posed

76
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.840
by the others, overwhelming our linear
defense systems.

77
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.920
Closing the mitigation gap requires moving
toward an interdependent three-layer

78
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.880
architecture. The first is the technical
layer.

79
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.640
We must replace sequence matching with
predictive screening, using AI

80
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.040
to analyze what a novel biological
structure will actually do.,

81
00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.440
rather than checking if we have seen it
before The

82
00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.280
second is the developer layer.

83
00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.800
We need statutory liability to force
safety to become a

84
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.040
legally binding business incentive rather
than a voluntary commitment.

85
00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.200
Finally, the international layer.

86
00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.640
While data is diffuse, the physical data
centers required to

87
00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.320
train frontier models are massive and
energy intensive.

88
00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.240
They provide verifiable choke points for
international oversight.

89
00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.040
These three layers are strictly
interdependent.

90
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.200
Liability requires a technical standard to
measure against, and international

91
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:16.520
agreements require the verifial choke
points that compute governance provides.

92
00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:21.080
Remove any single layer, and the entire
security structure collapses.

93
00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.720
The mitigation gap is a political failure.

94
00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:27.720
Policymakers lack the urgency to implement
new frameworks because experts

95
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.720
continue to falsely assure them that
catastrophic bio-risk is managed.

96
00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.280
When told that current safeguards provide
a 70-the hardest part

97
00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.000
of securing these biological advancements
isn't building better screening tools.

98
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.600
It's convincing decision makers that the
defenses they currently trust

99
00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.840
are dangerously inadequate.

